1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,480
Here's a prediction to make.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:04,480
Mix one mole per litre hydrochloric acid with

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:07,200
the same amount of sodium hydroxide, and you

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,000
get about minus fifty seven kilojoules per mole

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,120
of water.

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,320
Now swap in ethanoic acid, a weak acid.

7
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,720
Most people predict much less heat.

8
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:19,440
The measured value is about minus fifty six,

9
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:20,720
almost the same.

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:24,160
And hydrofluoric acid, also weak, gives about minus

11
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,200
sixty eight: more heat, not less.

12
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:29,360
So what really decides the heat?

13
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:32,286
Start with what you know.

14
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:35,766
In the neutralisation heat video, a strong acid

15
00:00:35,847 --> 00:00:38,406
and a strong base gave about minus fifty

16
00:00:38,487 --> 00:00:41,446
seven, because the only change is a hydrogen

17
00:00:41,527 --> 00:00:44,326
ion joining a hydroxide ion to make water.

18
00:00:44,967 --> 00:00:47,206
A weak acid is different before you even

19
00:00:47,287 --> 00:00:50,566
mix: most of its molecules are still whole,

20
00:00:50,647 --> 00:00:52,646
holding on to their protons.

21
00:00:52,727 --> 00:00:55,206
So the hydrogen ions the reaction needs aren't

22
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:56,086
free yet.

23
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,820
So what happens when hydroxide arrives?

24
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,340
It grabs the few hydronium ions that are

25
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:02,980
there.

26
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:06,420
That disturbs the acid's equilibrium, so, just as

27
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:10,020
Le Chatelier's principle predicts, more molecules ionise to

28
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:11,020
replace them.

29
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,500
Hydroxide grabs those too, and this keeps going

30
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:17,140
until every molecule has reacted.

31
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:19,420
So most of the ionising happens during the

32
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:22,100
reaction, not before you mix.

33
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:24,740
A weak acid doesn't react incompletely.

34
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:26,500
It just has one extra step.

35
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,100
We can count the energy in two steps,

36
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:33,060
using Hess's law from Year 11: the total

37
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:34,660
doesn't depend on the route.

38
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:38,340
Step one, the weak acid ionises.

39
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:41,940
Step two, the hydrogen ion and hydroxide ion

40
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:44,980
make water, about minus fifty seven, the same

41
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:46,100
as for a strong acid.

42
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:49,460
The measured enthalpy is the sum: minus fifty

43
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:52,180
seven, plus the ionisation step.

44
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,900
So the whole difference comes from step one,

45
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:57,380
and to find it, take the measured value,

46
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,380
take away minus fifty seven.

47
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:03,280
And step one isn't always uphill.

48
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:06,120
Pulling a proton off a molecule takes energy

49
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:08,240
in, because a bond breaks.

50
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,680
But the new ions are then surrounded by

51
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:14,120
water molecules, and that releases energy, just like

52
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:16,840
hydration when a salt dissolves in Module 5.

53
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,880
The ionisation enthalpy is the balance of the

54
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:22,520
two, so it can be positive, close to

55
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,480
zero, or negative, depending on the acid.

56
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,220
For ethanoic acid, the two nearly cancel: the

57
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,700
energy to pull the proton off is almost

58
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:36,540
exactly repaid when water surrounds the new ions.

59
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:39,500
Its ionisation step is close to zero, about

60
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,220
plus one, so the total is about minus

61
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:43,500
fifty six.

62
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:45,980
That's barely different from a strong acid, and

63
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:48,220
a foam cup experiment can hardly tell them

64
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:48,940
apart.

65
00:02:50,167 --> 00:02:52,686
Hydrofluoric acid is the surprise.

66
00:02:52,967 --> 00:02:55,926
The fluoride ion is very small, so water

67
00:02:56,007 --> 00:02:58,806
molecules crowd in tightly around it and release

68
00:02:58,887 --> 00:03:01,046
a lot of energy, more than it takes

69
00:03:01,127 --> 00:03:03,526
to break the hydrogen fluoride bond.

70
00:03:03,607 --> 00:03:07,046
So its ionisation step releases about eleven kilojoules

71
00:03:07,127 --> 00:03:09,606
per mole, and the total is about minus

72
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:10,806
sixty eight.

73
00:03:10,887 --> 00:03:12,486
It's still a weak acid.

74
00:03:12,807 --> 00:03:15,366
How far an acid ionises depends on more

75
00:03:15,447 --> 00:03:16,166
than the heat.

76
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:19,366
Weak bases follow the same rule.

77
00:03:19,767 --> 00:03:23,126
Hydrochloric acid with ammonia gives about minus fifty

78
00:03:23,187 --> 00:03:25,206
two kilojoules per mole of ammonia.

79
00:03:25,847 --> 00:03:28,246
Here we count the extra step as ammonia

80
00:03:28,327 --> 00:03:31,606
taking a proton from water to make hydroxide,

81
00:03:31,687 --> 00:03:34,326
and that step takes energy in, about five

82
00:03:34,487 --> 00:03:35,526
kilojoules per mole.

83
00:03:36,087 --> 00:03:39,286
So minus fifty seven plus five gives minus

84
00:03:39,327 --> 00:03:41,766
fifty two: less heat this time.

85
00:03:42,967 --> 00:03:45,126
So the heat doesn't tell you how strong

86
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:46,166
a weak acid is.

87
00:03:46,647 --> 00:03:49,766
Methanoic acid is ten times stronger than ethanoic

88
00:03:49,847 --> 00:03:53,126
acid, yet both give about minus fifty six.

89
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:55,926
And hydrofluoric acid releases the most heat, but

90
00:03:56,007 --> 00:03:57,526
it's still weak.

91
00:03:57,607 --> 00:03:59,686
So a value close to minus fifty seven

92
00:03:59,927 --> 00:04:02,966
doesn't prove an acid is strong: ethanoic acid

93
00:04:03,047 --> 00:04:04,326
would fool you.

94
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:06,966
To compare strength, use pH at the same

95
00:04:07,047 --> 00:04:09,606
concentration, or K a.

96
00:04:09,647 --> 00:04:12,166
The heat tells you about the ionising step,

97
00:04:12,247 --> 00:04:14,486
not how far the acid ionises.

98
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,260
So here's what to remember.

99
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:20,100
Same last step, different first step.

100
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,220
Every neutralisation can be counted as ending with

101
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:27,380
hydrogen ion plus hydroxide ion making water, about

102
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:28,980
minus fifty seven.

103
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:31,220
A weak acid or base adds a first

104
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:33,620
step, and its sign decides whether you get

105
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:36,340
less heat, about the same, or more.

106
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:38,206
Your turn.

107
00:04:38,767 --> 00:04:42,366
Ethanoic acid is neutralised by sodium hydroxide in

108
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:45,406
a polystyrene cup, and the temperature rises by

109
00:04:45,567 --> 00:04:47,006
six point seven degrees.

110
00:04:47,647 --> 00:04:50,606
Hydrochloric acid gives minus fifty seven under the

111
00:04:50,687 --> 00:04:51,646
same conditions.

112
00:04:52,287 --> 00:04:55,406
Using the amounts and assumptions on screen, calculate

113
00:04:55,447 --> 00:04:58,926
the enthalpy of neutralisation of ethanoic acid, then

114
00:04:59,087 --> 00:05:01,966
the enthalpy of its ionisation step, and comment

115
00:05:02,047 --> 00:05:03,406
on its size.

116
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:04,766
Pause here to answer.

117
00:05:15,167 --> 00:05:18,206
The mass is one hundred grams, so q

118
00:05:18,287 --> 00:05:20,886
is about two thousand eight hundred joules.

119
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:23,886
Divide by zero point zero five zero zero

120
00:05:24,047 --> 00:05:27,166
moles of water: delta H is minus fifty

121
00:05:27,247 --> 00:05:29,006
six kilojoules per mole.

122
00:05:29,327 --> 00:05:31,966
The ionisation step is measured minus the water

123
00:05:32,047 --> 00:05:35,166
step: minus fifty six take away minus fifty

124
00:05:35,247 --> 00:05:37,166
seven, about plus one.

125
00:05:37,567 --> 00:05:40,206
That's close to zero, within foam cup error.

126
00:05:40,687 --> 00:05:43,326
Subtracting the other way gets the sign backwards.

127
00:05:44,534 --> 00:05:45,173
Last one.

128
00:05:45,454 --> 00:05:48,693
Hydrogen cyanide is a weak acid, and its

129
00:05:48,774 --> 00:05:52,453
ionisation absorbs about forty five kilojoules per mole.

130
00:05:53,174 --> 00:05:55,893
Using minus fifty seven kilojoules per mole for

131
00:05:55,934 --> 00:05:58,693
a hydrogen ion and a hydroxide ion forming

132
00:05:58,774 --> 00:06:02,373
water, predict the enthalpy of neutralisation of hydrogen

133
00:06:02,534 --> 00:06:05,973
cyanide by sodium hydroxide, and explain why it

134
00:06:06,054 --> 00:06:08,173
differs from hydrochloric acid's.

135
00:06:08,454 --> 00:06:09,813
Pause here to answer.

136
00:06:20,234 --> 00:06:23,273
Add the two steps: minus fifty seven plus

137
00:06:23,394 --> 00:06:26,633
forty five is about minus twelve kilojoules per

138
00:06:26,714 --> 00:06:27,593
mole.

139
00:06:27,674 --> 00:06:29,993
That's much less heat, because most of the

140
00:06:30,074 --> 00:06:32,793
energy released by forming water is used up

141
00:06:32,834 --> 00:06:35,993
by the ionisation step of hydrogen cyanide, and

142
00:06:36,154 --> 00:06:38,873
hydrochloric acid has no such step.

143
00:06:39,114 --> 00:06:42,313
Writing minus fifty seven minus forty five counts

144
00:06:42,394 --> 00:06:44,473
the absorbed energy as released.

145
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:49,060
So for any weak acid or base neutralisation,

146
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:52,180
start from minus fifty seven for making water,

147
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,740
add the ionising step with its sign, whether

148
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:56,980
it's a weak acid giving up protons or

149
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,180
a weak base making hydroxide, and remember that

150
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:02,700
the reaction still goes to completion.

151
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:04,980
Don't use the heat to rank strength.

